      content: '**DEXA Interpretation**:\n- **T-score criteria** (WHO Classification):\n  - Normal: T-score >= -1.0\n  - Osteopenia: T-score -1.0 to -2.5\n  - Osteoporosis: T-score <= -2.5\n  - Severe/Established Osteoporosis: T-score <= -2.5 **with** a fragility fracture\n- This patient meets criteria for **severe osteoporosis**: T-score -3.1 (lumbar spine) with two fragility fractures (Colles\' fracture and now VCF)\n- The lumbar spine T-score may actually be **falsely elevated** by the compression fracture itself and degenerative changes — the femoral neck T-score (-2.7) may be more reliable for monitoring\n\n**FRAX Assessment** (Fracture Risk Assessment Tool):\n- FRAX is a WHO-developed algorithm that estimates the 10-year probability of major osteoporotic fracture and hip fracture based on clinical risk factors and femoral neck BMD\n- **This patient\'s risk factors for FRAX**: Age 72, female, prior fragility fracture, parental history of hip fracture, BMI 22 (low), no smoking, minimal alcohol\n- Treatment is recommended when: 10-year hip fracture risk >=3% or major osteoporotic fracture risk >=20%\n- This patient **does not need FRAX** to justify treatment — she has already sustained a fragility fracture with a T-score of -3.1. Treatment is **unequivocally indicated**.\n\n**Secondary Osteoporosis Workup**:\nBefore attributing her osteoporosis solely to postmenopausal/age-related causes, rule out secondary causes:\n1. **Laboratory studies**:\n   - **Calcium, phosphorus, albumin** (to detect hypercalcemia/hypocalcemia)\n   - **25-hydroxyvitamin D** (vitamin D deficiency is extremely common and worsens bone loss — she has a history of low levels)\n   - **PTH** (to rule out primary hyperparathyroidism)\n   - **TSH** (she is on levothyroxine — over-replacement with suppressed TSH accelerates bone loss)\n   - **Comprehensive metabolic panel** including creatinine (renal osteodystrophy)\n   - **CBC** (to screen for multiple myeloma, anemia)\n   - **Serum protein elect