asation risk\n   - **Timing**: If pain remains severe at 4-6 weeks despite conservative measures, refer for interventional evaluation. Some centers consider earlier intervention (2-3 weeks) for severe cases.\n   - **Prerequisite**: MRI to confirm marrow edema at the fracture level (confirms acute/subacute fracture) and to rule out retropulsion, cord compression, or malignancy\n   - **Contraindications**: Healed fracture without edema, retropulsed fragment with canal compromise, active infection, uncorrectable coagulopathy\n   - **Controversy**: The INVEST and VERTOS trials showed mixed results vs. sham procedure. However, for patients with severe pain and functional decline, the benefit is generally accepted.\n\n**Long-Term Osteoporosis Treatment**:\n\n1. **Bisphosphonate therapy** (once acute fracture management is initiated):\n   - Given her prior GI intolerance to oral alendronate, options include:\n     - **IV Zoledronic acid** (Reclast) 5 mg IV once yearly — preferred. Bypasses GI tract entirely. Most potent bisphosphonate. Can be initiated as early as 2 weeks after fracture.\n     - **Oral risedronate** (Actonel): Better GI tolerance profile than alendronate. If patient prefers oral.\n   - Alternative non-bisphosphonate: **Denosumab** (Prolia) 60 mg SQ every 6 months — if bisphosphonates contraindicated or not tolerated. Caution: rebound bone loss and vertebral fractures upon discontinuation.\n\n2. **Anabolic therapy** consideration:\n   - Given two fragility fractures and T-score -3.1, this patient is at **very high fracture risk**\n   - **Teriparatide** (Forteo) 20 mcg SQ daily or **Abaloparatide** (Tymlos) 80 mcg SQ daily — PTH analog that builds new bone (anabolic) rather than just preventing resorption\n   - **Romosozumab** (Evenity) 210 mg SQ monthly for 12 months — sclerostin inhibitor with dual anabolic/antiresorptive effect. Most potent reduction in fracture risk. Caution: cardiovascular risk (avoid in patients with recent MI or stroke)\n   - Current guidelines increasingly support **anabolic-first therapy** in very high-risk patients, followed by transition to bisphosphonate or denosumab\n\n3. **Calcium and Vitamin D**:\n   - Vitamin D: Check 25-OH vitamin D level. Target >30 ng/mL. Supplement with vitamin D3 1000-2000 IU daily (higher if deficient).\n   - Calcium: Total intake 1200 mg/day (diet + supplement). Prefer dietary calcium when possible. Limit supplements to 500 mg at a time for absorption.\n\n4. **Medication deprescribing**:\n   - Taper and discontinue omeprazole if possible (try H2 blocker or lifestyle modifications for GERD)\n   - Check TSH and adjust levothyroxine if over-replaced\n\n5. **Rehabilitation**:\n   - Once acute pain subsides (2-4 weeks), begin progressive exercise program:\n   - Weight-bearing exercise, resistance training (back extensors), balance training\n   - Avoid spinal flexion exercises (no sit-ups, no toe touches)\n   - Goal: Prevent further fractures through strength, balance, and fall prevention',
