usal women **<65** with risk factors (use FRAX to identify those with 10-year risk equivalent to a 65-year-old white woman — approximately 9.3%)\n   - **Insufficient evidence** to recommend screening in men (Grade I)\n\n2. **ISCD (International Society for Clinical Densitometry)**:\n   - Women >=65 and men >=70\n   - Younger postmenopausal women and men 50-69 with risk factors\n   - Anyone with a fragility fracture\n   - Anyone on medications associated with bone loss (glucocorticoids, aromatase inhibitors, androgen deprivation)\n\n3. **Monitoring**:\n   - Repeat DEXA every **1-2 years** during treatment to assess response\n   - Measure at the same site with the same machine for consistency\n   - Bone turnover markers (CTX) can assess treatment response earlier (3-6 months)\n\n4. **Fracture Liaison Service (FLS)**:\n   - An FLS is a systematic, coordinated model of care that identifies patients with fragility fractures and ensures they receive appropriate osteoporosis evaluation and treatment\n   - Studies show that without an FLS, up to **80% of fragility fracture patients never receive osteoporosis treatment** — a critical care gap\n   - If your institution has an FLS, this patient should be enrolled. If not, advocate for developing one.',
