t.\n\nThat said, I also have options that bypass the concerns you have about oral bisphosphonates. We could consider an annual IV infusion of zoledronic acid — just once a year, no GI side effects. Or we could consider an entirely different class of medication that actually builds new bone rather than just preventing loss. I would like to find the option that you are most comfortable with.\n\nWhat matters most to me is that you are protected. I do not want to see you back here with another fracture — or worse. Can we explore these options together?\'\n\n**Key Communication Strategies**:\n- **Acknowledged her concerns as legitimate** rather than dismissing them\n- **Provided specific risk data** to counter vague fears with concrete numbers\n- **Contextualized the risk** by comparing rare side effects to the very real risk of future fractures\n- **Used her personal history** (mother\'s hip fracture) to make the risk tangible\n- **Offered alternatives** to address the specific barriers (GI intolerance, fear of bisphosphonates)\n- **Respected autonomy** by framing the conversation as a partnership ("Can we explore these options together?")\n- **Did not use scare tactics** but was direct about the consequences of non-treatment',
